00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:14.040
first strategy.

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:16.530
And what's really in that?

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:19.830
From our experience, we have
seen three high level things,

00:07:19.830 --> 00:07:24.630
which take the
most pain in terms

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:26.880
of trying to become digital.

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:31.860
The first one is obviously
how do I easily and securely

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:35.730
connect to cloud services,
fast applications,

00:07:35.730 --> 00:07:40.130
and in many cases, on-premise
applications as well?

00:07:40.130 --> 00:07:43.460
The second piece that
becomes really important,

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:49.120
once you've done that, how do
I easily take those assets,

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:52.600
turn them into highly
curated APIs that can

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:55.600
deliver the right experience?

00:07:55.600 --> 00:07:58.780
And more importantly, do
not forget the application

00:07:58.780 --> 00:07:59.860
developer.

00:07:59.860 --> 00:08:03.070
You want to ensure that the
application developer has

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:07.060
the right set of tools,
modular capabilities,

00:08:07.060 --> 00:08:11.750
so they can reuse those assets
and turn out and churn out

00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:14.320
API much faster.

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:17.130
So for that, let's
kind of break down

00:08:17.130 --> 00:08:21.500
the anatomy of how do you
really access a cloud service.

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:24.230
It turns out there are a
set of repeatable steps

00:08:24.230 --> 00:08:27.200
that has to be done
over and over again.

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:30.690
So let's try and just walk
through them very quickly.

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:33.799
So let's say I decide I need
to access some kind of a cloud

00:08:33.799 --> 00:08:34.760
service.

00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:37.669
But first, as an
API developer, I

00:08:37.669 --> 00:08:41.419
have to figure out where
do I store the credentials

00:08:41.419 --> 00:08:42.770
with that service.

00:08:42.770 --> 00:08:45.170
Could be a service
account, could be just

00:08:45.170 --> 00:08:46.430
a simple username, password.

00:08:46.430 --> 00:08:49.580
I need a secure
mechanism to persist it.

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:52.460
Secondly, once they
have that, then I

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:55.540
need to go talk to
a token endpoint,

00:08:55.540 --> 00:08:57.910
passing in those credentials,
so I can actually

00:08:57.910 --> 00:09:01.750
get a token to that service.

00:09:01.750 --> 00:09:05.830
Then I have the same issue that
I need to go manage the token,

00:09:05.830 --> 00:09:10.350
persist it, and also worry about
the exploration and refresh

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:11.480
cycles.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:14.500
But once I've done
that, finally, I

00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:18.200
can go call my cloud service.

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:19.930
And although that's
a set of things

00:09:19.930 --> 00:09:22.330
which you have to do
to access a service,

00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:24.650
we feel it's very error prone.

00:09:24.650 --> 00:09:26.500
If you have one
API developer who's

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:28.690
trying to do that to
access the service

00:09:28.690 --> 00:09:32.120
and suddenly they need
to scale to provide that

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:34.930
to many different
services, it just

00:09:34.930 --> 00:09:37.660
becomes extremely repetitive.

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:39.380
It's time consuming.

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:45.017
It's not easy to have the API
developer wire those things

00:09:45.017 --> 00:09:45.850
over and over again.

00:09:45.850 --> 00:09:49.750
And more importantly,
the [INAUDIBLE] steps

00:09:49.750 --> 00:09:54.314
tend to build up over time and
causes a loss in productivity.

00:09:54.314 --> 00:09:55.730
The other thing
we constantly hear

00:09:55.730 --> 00:10:00.610
is administrators are not
very comfortable letting

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:03.823
developers manage and
deal with credentials.

00:10:03.823 --> 00:10:08.020
OK, they want to have the
right separation of concerns.

00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:11.980
They want to be able to
have designated roles

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:15.280
who can manage credentials, and
more importantly, have the API

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:19.060
developers go off and do their
stuff, which is go access

00:10:19.060 --> 00:10:25.060
those APIs or services and
build some nice experiences.

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:29.640
So let's see how we
can get around this.

